{
    "title": "Bill of Rights Day, 2025",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 18, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-18/pdf/2025-23416.pdf",
    "summary": "The President issued a proclamation declaring December 15, 2025 as Bill of Rights Day, celebrating the historic protections of individual freedoms and calling on schools and communities to mark the occasion. The message honors the origins of the first ten amendments, highlights core liberties like free speech, religious freedom, due process, and the right to a fair trial, and frames these rights as the enduring foundation of American self-government.\n\nThe proclamation looks back to the debates of the Founders, citing James Madison and Thomas Jefferson, and lists the rights the states ratified in 1791. It stresses that ink on paper is not enough and ties the health of these freedoms to civic virtue, checks and balances, and limits on federal power. It also connects the commemoration to the 250th anniversary of American independence in 2026.\n\nAlongside the historical reflections, the document includes current political themes. It says the administration is working to restore the rule of law, fight back against what it calls left-wing lawfare, and \u201cdrain the swamp,\u201d positioning the federal government as accountable to the people. The proclamation formally invites teachers, educators, and school administrators to observe the day with lessons and events.\n\nWho stands to benefit most are educators, students, civic groups, and communities that want a timely prompt to teach and reflect on constitutional rights. Supporters who value public recognition of the Bill of Rights and an emphasis on state and local roles may also welcome the tone and timing.\n\nThose who may feel uneasy include readers who see the partisan language as shifting a unifying civic anniversary into political territory. Public servants might view the \u201cswamp\u201d framing as disparaging, and some civil liberties advocates may question whether the broad claims align with current practice. The proclamation is symbolic and does not change law, but it sets a message about how the administration views the Constitution, rights, and the role of government heading into the nation\u2019s semiquincentennial.",
